        "When an RT core's hardware traversal unit determines that a ray has intersected a leaf node of the Bounding Volume Hierarchy, it executes the fundamental geometric collision test of computer graphics: The Ray-Triangle Intersection. In modern silicon, this is executed by the M\u00f6ller-Trumbore Algorithm, which evaluates ray-triangle intersection directly from the triangle's three vertex coordinates without requiring the precomputation of plane equations.",
        "The M\u00f6ller-Trumbore algorithm expresses any point on a triangle as P(u, v) = (1 - u - v) * V0 + u * V1 + v * V2, where V0, V1, V2 are the triangle vertices and u, v are Barycentric Coordinates. Setting the ray equation R(t) = O + t * D equal to P(u, v) yields a linear system of three equations with three unknowns: [-D, V1 - V0, V2 - V0] * [t, u, v]^T = O - V0. By applying Cramer's Rule, the algorithm solves for hit distance t and barycentric coordinates (u, v) using pure vector cross and dot products.",
